Time: O(log n) | Space: O(log n) call stack
Concept
This is the same halving logic as the iterative version, but expressed as a function calling itself on a narrower
[lo, hi] range instead of updating loop variables. Making `hi` default to `None` (resolved to `len(arr) - 1` on the
first call) lets callers invoke `binary_search_rec(arr, target)` without manually supplying the initial bounds.
def binary_search_rec(arr, target, lo=0, hi=None):
if hi is None:
hi = len(arr) - 1
if lo > hi:
return -1
mid = (lo + hi) // 2
if arr[mid] == target:
return mid
elif arr[mid] < target:
return binary_search_rec(arr, target, mid + 1, hi)
else:
return binary_search_rec(arr, target, lo, mid - 1)
Why the Complexity Holds
The number of recursive calls is identical to the number of iterations in the loop version — O(log n) — since the
search window still halves each time. The difference is purely in *where* that state lives: the iterative version
keeps `lo` and `hi` in local variables reused across a loop (O(1) space), while the recursive version keeps a
separate stack frame for every call until the base case, giving O(log n) space instead of O(1).
Transition
Standard binary search finds *one* matching index arbitrarily. The final searching example refines the same
halving strategy to find the *first* and *last* index of a repeated value — useful whenever a sorted array
contains duplicates and the full range of matches matters, not just any single hit.

Time: O(h), h = tree height | Space: O(h)
Concept
Inserting into a BST walks from the root, moving left whenever the new value is smaller than the current node
and right whenever it is larger, until an empty spot (None) is reached — that is where the new node is attached.
Search follows the exact same left/right decision rule to locate a value or confirm its absence, which is the direct
benefit of the BST ordering invariant: at each node, an entire subtree can be ruled out with a single comparison.
class TreeNode:
def __init__(self, val):
self.val = val
self.left = None
self.right = None
def insert(root, val):
if root is None:
return TreeNode(val)
if val < root.val:
root.left = insert(root.left, val)
else:
root.right = insert(root.right, val)
return root
def search(root, val):
if root is None or root.val == val:
return root
if val < root.val:
return search(root.left, val)
return search(root.right, val)
Why the Complexity Holds
Both insert and search follow a single root-to-leaf path, and at each node they do O(1) work before recursing
into exactly one child, so their cost is proportional to the tree's height h — O(h) time, and O(h) call-stack space
for the recursion. For a balanced tree, h = O(log n), giving the same asymptotic performance as binary search
on a sorted array; but if values are inserted in already-sorted order, the tree degenerates into a straight chain
with h = O(n), and both operations collapse to O(n) — the motivation for self-balancing BST variants (not
covered here) such as AVL or red-black trees.
Transition
Insertion and search only visit a single path. The next example, traversal, instead visits *every* node in the tree,
and the order in which it does so — inorder, preorder, or postorder — determines what property of the tree is
revealed.
